# Fixed 2026-11-20, defect 2522's row *a jump through a buffer J never saw*,
# lane-signals (panel 251's R1: it owes words of its own): a correct program
# saves a buffer, copies it, and its handler jumps out of itself through the
# copy; the signal over, it calls the function from far deeper.
# Symptom: refused with *was called from inside a signal handler: C called it
# while handling a signal*, false of this program (40 runs of 40 on this Mac
# at `-O0` and `-O2`, 20 of 20 at `-O0` in Linux arm64).
# Cause: the copy is a buffer the runtime never saw filled, so it cannot tell
# a jump out of the handler from one within it, and keeps the handler as
# running (a miss errs toward a told refusal). The refusal stands; its words
# now say what the runtime knows: a jump it could not follow.
extern "fixedbugs-2522-a-jump-out-of-a-handler-through-a-copied-buffer.h"
    function copied_install(sig: i32) -> i32
    function copied_escape(sig: i32) -> i32
    function copied_call(cb: (function(i64) -> i64)) -> i64

extern "signal.h"
    constant SIGUSR1: i32

function answer(n: i64) -> i64
    return n + 1

function main()
    print("installed ", copied_install(sig: SIGUSR1))
    print("escaped ", copied_escape(sig: SIGUSR1))
    print("called ", copied_call(cb: answer))
